/* Contenido profundo de ob_conversational (borrador para revisión del equipo curricular; ver "verify" por unidad). */
window.EL_DEEP = window.EL_DEEP || {};
Object.assign(window.EL_DEEP, {"us:world_language:conversational:Oct": {"reading": ["When we open a Bible in our own language, we rarely stop to think that this simple act was once costly, dangerous and, in some places, forbidden. The story of the Spanish Bible begins in the sixteenth century, a time of deep religious change across Europe. The Protestant Reformation, which started in the German lands in 1517, spread ideas that questioned many practices of the medieval church and insisted that ordinary believers should be able to read the Scriptures for themselves. In Spain, then ruled by a powerful Catholic monarchy that saw religious unity as the foundation of the state, these ideas were received with suspicion. The Spanish Inquisition, founded in 1478 under the Catholic Monarchs, was the institution charged with guarding that unity, and it could investigate, imprison and condemn people accused of holding unorthodox beliefs. To understand the Bible in Castilian, therefore, we must understand both the courage of its translators and the tense world in which they lived.", "Casiodoro de Reina, who lived from about 1520 to 1594, was a Spaniard who joined the monastery of San Isidoro del Campo, a Hieronymite house near Seville. Monks in such communities lived by a rule of prayer, study and work, and Reina became a learned man who knew Latin and later mastered Hebrew and Greek. According to the traditional account, a small circle of monks at San Isidoro came to hold reforming convictions after reading the Scriptures and Protestant writings. Around the middle of the 1550s, when the Inquisition grew more attentive to such circles, several of them decided to flee. Reina left Spain and began a long exile that took him through several European cities, among them Geneva, Frankfurt, London, Antwerp and Basel. He worked as a pastor to Spanish-speaking refugees, but he never abandoned the project that gave meaning to his life: giving his people the whole Bible in their own language. Exile meant poverty, family separation and constant uncertainty.", "A Spanish Bible already existed in some forms before Reina. Medieval translations, usually made from the Latin Vulgate, had circulated in limited ways, and a Spanish Jewish tradition had produced important translations of the Hebrew Scriptures. What Reina wanted was a complete Bible translated, as far as possible, from the original Hebrew and Greek, while consulting earlier versions, including Latin and other translations, to compare difficult passages. He worked for roughly a dozen years. In 1569 the work was printed in Basel, a Swiss city famous for its printers and its freedom from censorship. The printer was Thomas Guarin, and the book carried an emblem of a bear reaching for a honeycomb in a tree. Because of that image the translation became known as the Biblia del Oso, the Bear Bible. The bear and the honey are often read as a picture of Scripture: sweet food worth reaching for.", "Why did a Bible in Castilian matter so much? First, because language is the doorway to understanding. A text read only in Latin remains closed to those who have not studied that language, however much they may respect it. A translation opens the door to farmers, craftsmen, mothers and children. Second, because printing had changed the world. The printing press, developed in Europe in the fifteenth century, made books cheaper and let ideas cross borders in months instead of generations. Third, because translation is an act of service: the translator humbles himself so that another person may hear God's word as a neighbour speaks. It is worth noting that Catholic scholars also translated the Bible into modern languages in later centuries, and that respect for the dignity of every people and every Christian tradition is part of studying this history honestly.", "The Biblia del Oso did not end its journey in 1569. Later editors corrected and refined its language, and it became the foundation of what we call the Reina-Valera tradition, still read today in many Spanish-speaking churches across Spain and the Americas. Its vocabulary has shaped how generations of believers pray and speak of their faith. For the student of Spanish, this is a valuable lesson in language history: a single translation can influence the rhythm and the vocabulary of an entire community. Reina's life also reminds us of a verse many translators have loved, Psalm 119:105: your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path. In this unit you will practise narrating Reina's story with the past tenses. Use the preterite for completed events (Reina nació, huyó, tradujo, publicó) and the imperfect for background and habit (vivía en un monasterio, estudiaba las Escrituras, la gente no podía leer la Biblia). Telling history well means choosing the right tense to show what happened and what the world was like at the time."], "glossary": [{"term": "la traducción", "def": "Translation: the transfer of a text from one language to another; Reina worked from Hebrew and Greek."}, {"term": "el exilio", "def": "Exile: forced or necessary absence from one's homeland, as in Reina's flight from Spain."}, {"term": "la imprenta", "def": "The printing press (also a printing house): the technology that made books cheaper and ideas easier to spread."}, {"term": "el monje", "def": "Monk: a man who lives in a religious community under a rule of prayer, study and work."}, {"term": "las Escrituras", "def": "The Scriptures: the Bible, the sacred writings of the Christian faith."}, {"term": "el siglo", "def": "Century: a period of one hundred years; Reina lived in the sixteenth century (el siglo XVI)."}, {"term": "la Biblia del Oso", "def": "The Bear Bible: the 1569 Basel edition of Reina's translation, named for the bear on the printer's emblem."}, {"term": "el pretérito / el imperfecto", "def": "The two main Spanish past tenses: preterite for completed events, imperfect for background, habit and description."}], "practice": [{"q": "Explain in your own words why a Bible in Castilian was important in the sixteenth century. Give at least two reasons.", "feedback": "A strong answer mentions that language opens understanding: ordinary people could not read Latin, so a translation let them read the Scriptures for themselves. It can also mention the printing press, which made books affordable, and the idea of translation as service. Both reasons connect to the Reformation conviction that every believer should have access to the text."}, {"q": "Narrate Reina's story in Spanish in four sentences, using at least two preterite verbs and two imperfect verbs.", "feedback": "A model answer: 'Casiodoro de Reina vivía en un monasterio cerca de Sevilla y estudiaba las Escrituras. Como la situación era peligrosa, huyó de España. En el exilio tradujo la Biblia del hebreo y del griego. En 1569 la publicó en Basilea.' Preterite verbs (huyó, tradujo, publicó) show completed actions; imperfect verbs (vivía, estudiaba, era) give the background."}, {"q": "Why does the text say that exile was not a holiday? What does this teach us about the cost of translation?", "feedback": "Exile meant poverty, family separation and uncertainty. The point is that Reina's translation was the fruit of years of sacrifice, not an easy project. This helps us value the Bible we hold and respect people who risk comfort or safety for their convictions, without turning the story into hatred of any person or group today."}, {"q": "Write three open questions in Spanish that a journalist could ask Casiodoro de Reina (use ¿Por qué...?, ¿Cómo...? and ¿Qué pasó cuando...?).", "feedback": "Examples: '¿Por qué decidió traducir la Biblia al castellano?' '¿Cómo trabajó desde el exilio?' '¿Qué pasó cuando se imprimió la Biblia en Basilea?' Good questions are open (they cannot be answered with sí or no), use a question word with the correct accent, and are in the preterite when they ask about completed events."}], "quiz": [{"q": "In what year was the Biblia del Oso printed in Basel?", "opts": [{"t": "1569", "ok": true}, {"t": "1517", "ok": false}, {"t": "1602", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Why is the translation called the Biblia del Oso?", "opts": [{"t": "Because Reina was born in a place called Oso", "ok": false}, {"t": "Because it was first printed in a forest town", "ok": false}, {"t": "Because of the bear on the printer's emblem", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence correctly uses the imperfect to describe the background of the story?", "opts": [{"t": "Reina vivió en un monasterio y tradujo ayer.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Reina vivía en un monasterio cerca de Sevilla.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Reina vivirá en un monasterio cerca de Sevilla.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "According to the reading, what languages did Reina work from for his translation?", "opts": [{"t": "Only Latin", "ok": false}, {"t": "Hebrew and Greek, consulting earlier versions", "ok": true}, {"t": "Only English and German", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which statement reflects the respectful approach to this history proposed in the reading?", "opts": [{"t": "Catholic scholars also translated the Bible, and every tradition deserves respect", "ok": true}, {"t": "Only one tradition ever translated the Bible", "ok": false}, {"t": "Anyone in Spain at that time was an enemy of the Bible", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Reina c. 1520-1594: confirm birth place and dates with a history source; Montemolín is sometimes mentioned but is not stated in the text.", "Departure from Spain around the mid-1550s and the route of exile (Geneva, Frankfurt, London, Antwerp, Basel): confirm order and dates.", "Basel 1569, printer Thomas Guarin and the bear emblem: confirm; also confirm the duration of the work (about twelve years).", "Spanish Inquisition founded 1478; Protestant Reformation traditionally dated from 1517.", "Confirm the statement that Catholic scholars translated the Bible into modern languages later, and avoid naming editions without a source."], "reading_es": ["Casiodoro de Reina nació hacia 1520 y murió en 1594. Era monje en el monasterio de San Isidoro del Campo, cerca de Sevilla. En aquella época, en el siglo XVI, Europa vivía grandes cambios religiosos. La Reforma protestante, que comenzó en 1517, defendía que cada creyente debía poder leer las Escrituras. En España, la Inquisición, fundada en 1478, vigilaba las creencias de las personas. Reina estudiaba la Biblia con otros monjes, pero la situación era peligrosa, de modo que huyó de España hacia mediados de la década de 1550.", "Durante su largo exilio, Reina vivió en varias ciudades europeas, entre ellas Ginebra, Fráncfort, Londres, Amberes y Basilea. Allí trabajó como pastor de otros refugiados españoles y, mientras tanto, traducía la Biblia a partir de los textos originales en hebreo y griego, comparándolos con versiones anteriores. El exilio era duro: había pobreza, incertidumbre y separación de la familia. A pesar de todo, no abandonó su proyecto.", "En 1569 se publicó en Basilea la primera Biblia completa en castellano que se imprimió a partir de esos textos originales. El impresor, Thomas Guarin, usaba como emblema un oso que alcanza un panal de miel; por eso la obra se llama la Biblia del Oso. La imprenta, inventada en Europa en el siglo XV, hizo posible que los libros fueran más baratos y que las ideas viajaran con rapidez.", "¿Por qué fue tan importante una Biblia en castellano? Porque el idioma es la puerta de la comprensión. Un campesino o una madre no podían leer un texto en latín, pero sí podían entender su propia lengua. Además, traducir es un acto de servicio: el traductor se hace humilde para que otros escuchen la Palabra con claridad. Conviene recordar que también los estudiosos católicos tradujeron la Biblia a las lenguas modernas, y que debemos hablar de esta historia con respeto hacia todas las tradiciones cristianas y todos los pueblos.", "La Biblia del Oso fue revisada después y se convirtió en la base de la tradición Reina-Valera, que todavía se usa en muchas iglesias de habla hispana. Como estudiantes de español, podemos aprender de esta historia a narrar en pasado: usamos el pretérito para los hechos terminados (nació, huyó, tradujo, publicó) y el imperfecto para describir las circunstancias (vivía, estudiaba, no podía). El Salmo 119:105 resume el sentido de este trabajo: lámpara es a mis pies tu palabra, y lumbrera a mi camino."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:Nov": {"reading": ["Cipriano de Valera, born around 1531 and dead in 1602, belongs to the same story as Casiodoro de Reina, and the two are remembered together in the name of the Reina-Valera Bible. Like Reina, Valera was linked to the monastery of San Isidoro del Campo near Seville, and like him he left Spain because the Inquisition had begun to investigate the circle of reform-minded believers to which they belonged. His exile took him to Geneva and then to England, where he studied and lived for many years, teaching and writing in a country that had broken with Rome and offered refuge to many foreign Protestants. From a distance of more than two decades, he turned back toward the language of his childhood. Valera understood that a single translation, however courageous, needs to be improved by careful readers, and that the community of faith is served when scholars correct and polish earlier work.", "In 1602, the year of his death, Valera published in Amsterdam a revision of Reina's Bible. He compared the Biblia del Oso with other versions and with the original languages, corrected passages, adjusted spelling and made decisions about the books of the Old Testament known as apocryphal or deuterocanonical, which Reina had placed in a separate section and which Valera left out. Because of this revision, the translation took the name Reina-Valera. The compound name is a good example of how scholarship works: a text is rarely the work of one person, but of a chain of people who receive, test and hand on what they have been given. Later centuries produced further revisions of the Reina-Valera text, which today is read in many forms among Spanish-speaking Protestants.", "To understand why these men left Spain, we must say something honest about the Inquisition. The Spanish Inquisition was a court established in 1478 by the Catholic Monarchs, Isabella and Ferdinand, to examine the orthodoxy of the faith of the baptized. It operated through investigations, interrogations, secrecy and, in serious cases, public ceremonies called autos de fe, where sentences were announced. In the 1550s and 1560s, small groups of believers who had adopted Protestant ideas were prosecuted in cities such as Seville and Valladolid. Some were imprisoned, some were executed and others, like Reina and Valera, escaped abroad. Historians still debate the exact numbers and the severity of the institution in different periods, and the subject has often been exaggerated by those who wished to defend or attack Spain. A careful student separates documented facts from legend.", "How should a Christian speak about these events today? Truthfully, and without hatred. Truthfully, because the sufferings of those who were persecuted were real, and covering them with silence would be unjust. Without hatred, because the people who live now in Spain, whether Catholic, Protestant, Orthodox, Jewish, Muslim or without religion, did not commit those acts. Many Catholics today deeply regret the use of force in matters of conscience, and the Catholic Church itself has offered public acknowledgments of past wrongs in the modern era, while Protestants in other places also persecuted dissenters, and history shows that no tradition is free from this temptation. The Bible gives a clear guide: speak the truth in love, Paul wrote in Ephesians 4:15, and do not repay evil for evil.", "The verse for this unit, Acts 17:11, praises the people of Berea, who received the message eagerly and examined the Scriptures every day to see whether what they heard was true. It gives us a model for our own study: neither credulous nor suspicious, but attentive, willing to check sources and to read for ourselves. In Spanish class, this unit invites you to practise connectors that structure an argument: en primer lugar, además, sin embargo, por lo tanto, aunque. You will also learn to present facts in the past (fue fundada en 1478, huyeron de España, publicó una revisión) and to debate with respect, offering evidence and listening to the other side. A debate that ends in contempt has failed, even if one side wins the vote."], "glossary": [{"term": "la Inquisición", "def": "The Inquisition: the Church-and-crown court founded in Spain in 1478 to examine the orthodoxy of the baptized."}, {"term": "el reformado", "def": "Reformed or reform-minded believer: someone who adopted Reformation ideas, such as the Seville circle."}, {"term": "la revisión", "def": "Revision: the careful correction of an existing translation; Valera's 1602 work revised Reina's Bible."}, {"term": "el auto de fe", "def": "Auto de fe: a public ceremony in which the Inquisition announced sentences."}, {"term": "la persecución", "def": "Persecution: systematic harassment or punishment of people because of their beliefs."}, {"term": "sin embargo", "def": "However: a connector that introduces a contrast, essential for balanced argument in Spanish."}, {"term": "por lo tanto", "def": "Therefore: a connector that introduces a conclusion drawn from previous facts."}, {"term": "la conciencia", "def": "Conscience: a person's inner moral judgment, especially in matters of faith."}], "practice": [{"q": "What is the origin of the name Reina-Valera? Explain in two or three sentences.", "feedback": "The name combines the translator of the 1569 Bible, Casiodoro de Reina, and Cipriano de Valera, who published a revision in Amsterdam in 1602. The name signals that the text is the result of two stages of work. It reminds us that translations are improved over time by careful scholars."}, {"q": "Why does the reading say that a student of history must separate documented facts from legend about the Inquisition? Give one example of how you would do it.", "feedback": "Historians disagree about numbers and about how severe the institution was in different periods, and some writers have exaggerated to defend or to attack Spain. A good student checks several reliable sources, identifies who wrote them and when, and states clearly what is documented and what is uncertain. For example, instead of repeating a large number found online, one would say that exact figures are debated."}, {"q": "How can a Christian speak about the Inquisition 'with truth and without hatred'? Use one connector such as sin embargo or por lo tanto.", "feedback": "A model answer: 'Los perseguidos sufrieron de verdad; sin embargo, los católicos de hoy no son responsables de aquellos hechos. Por lo tanto, debemos hablar con respeto.' The answer should honour the victims, avoid collective blame, and follow Ephesians 4:15, speaking the truth in love."}, {"q": "Write two sentences in the past in Spanish about Valera, one with the preterite and one with the imperfect.", "feedback": "Examples: 'Valera publicó una revisión de la Biblia en Ámsterdam en 1602.' 'Valera vivía en Inglaterra y estudiaba mucho.' The preterite marks a completed action with a time reference, while the imperfect describes an ongoing situation in the past."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What did Cipriano de Valera publish in Amsterdam in 1602?", "opts": [{"t": "The first Bible ever printed in Spanish", "ok": false}, {"t": "A revision of Reina's Bible", "ok": true}, {"t": "A Latin dictionary", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "When was the Spanish Inquisition founded, according to the reading?", "opts": [{"t": "1478", "ok": true}, {"t": "1569", "ok": false}, {"t": "1602", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which connector introduces a contrast?", "opts": [{"t": "Por lo tanto", "ok": false}, {"t": "En primer lugar", "ok": false}, {"t": "Sin embargo", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What attitude toward modern people does the reading recommend when discussing the Inquisition?", "opts": [{"t": "Blame all Spaniards today", "ok": false}, {"t": "Avoid speaking of the past completely", "ok": false}, {"t": "Speak with truth and without hatred toward people today", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What does Acts 17:11 praise in the people of Berea?", "opts": [{"t": "They refused to listen to anyone", "ok": false}, {"t": "They examined the Scriptures daily to check what they heard", "ok": true}, {"t": "They translated the Bible into Latin", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Valera c. 1531-1602: confirm birth place and details of his years in England (Geneva, Cambridge or Oxford).", "Amsterdam 1602 revision: confirm treatment of the apocryphal or deuterocanonical books and that Valera left them out of the main text.", "Inquisition founded 1478; Seville and Valladolid prosecutions in the 1550s-60s: confirm with a history source and avoid giving counts of victims.", "Statement that the Catholic Church has publicly acknowledged past wrongs: confirm the form and date (for example the 2000 Jubilee) before stating it in class.", "Ephesians 4:15 quotation and reference."], "reading_es": ["Cipriano de Valera nació hacia 1531 y murió en 1602. Como Casiodoro de Reina, estuvo vinculado al monasterio de San Isidoro del Campo, cerca de Sevilla, y tuvo que abandonar España porque la Inquisición investigaba a los creyentes que simpatizaban con la Reforma. Vivió muchos años en el exilio, sobre todo en Inglaterra, donde estudió, escribió y enseñó. Desde allí no olvidó su idioma ni a su pueblo, y dedicó muchos años de trabajo paciente a mejorar la Biblia en castellano para que todos pudieran leerla.", "En 1602 Valera publicó en Ámsterdam una revisión de la Biblia de Reina. Comparó la Biblia del Oso con otras versiones y con los textos originales, corrigió pasajes y ajustó la ortografía. Gracias a esta revisión, la traducción recibió el nombre de Reina-Valera. Esto nos enseña que un texto importante suele ser el resultado del trabajo de muchas personas que lo reciben, lo examinan y lo transmiten.", "La Inquisición española fue un tribunal fundado en 1478 por los Reyes Católicos para examinar la fe de los bautizados. En las décadas de 1550 y 1560 se procesó a pequeños grupos de creyentes reformados en ciudades como Sevilla y Valladolid. Algunos fueron encarcelados o ejecutados, y otros huyeron al extranjero. Los historiadores todavía discuten las cifras exactas, y por eso debemos separar con cuidado los hechos documentados de las leyendas.", "¿Cómo debemos hablar de estos hechos hoy? Con verdad y sin odio. Con verdad, porque el sufrimiento de los perseguidos fue real. Sin odio, porque los españoles de hoy, sean católicos, protestantes, judíos, musulmanes o no creyentes, no cometieron aquellos actos. Muchos católicos lamentan hoy el uso de la fuerza en cuestiones de conciencia, y también hubo protestantes que persiguieron a quienes pensaban distinto. La Biblia nos enseña a decir la verdad en amor (Efesios 4:15).", "El versículo de esta unidad, Hechos 17:11, elogia a los habitantes de Berea porque escudriñaban cada día las Escrituras. Es un modelo para el estudio: ni ingenuos ni desconfiados, sino atentos y dispuestos a comprobar las fuentes. En clase practicaremos conectores para argumentar (en primer lugar, además, sin embargo, por lo tanto) y debatiremos con respeto, aportando datos y escuchando a los demás."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:Dic": {"reading": ["The nineteenth century was a great age of Bible distribution. Improvements in printing, cheaper paper and faster ships made it possible, for the first time, to produce Scriptures in large numbers and send them across oceans. Out of this context grew the Bible societies, organizations that existed for a single purpose: to translate, print and distribute the Bible at a price ordinary people could afford. The British and Foreign Bible Society, founded in London in 1804, became one of the most influential, and other societies appeared in Scotland, the United States and elsewhere. Their work was supported by the gifts of many believers who never saw the countries their Bibles would reach. For the Spanish-speaking world, which stretched from Spain across the Atlantic to the new republics of South America, this movement opened a door that had been difficult to enter for centuries.", "The people who carried the books were called colporteurs, from a French word for a travelling seller. A colporteur walked, rode a mule, sailed on a river boat or took passage on a ship, carrying a pack of Bibles, Gospels and small tracts. He sold them cheaply or gave them away, spoke with anyone who would listen and often slept in humble inns or in the homes of strangers. The work was tiring and sometimes dangerous. Roads were poor, distances were enormous, and the colporteur could meet indifference, curiosity, warm hospitality or open hostility. Yet many readers first encountered the Scriptures in this way. A farmer who had never owned a book could buy a New Testament for a few coins and read it by the light of a lamp, and a family could gather to hear it aloud. In a region where many people could not read, the colporteur often read to them.", "One famous example is George Borrow, an English writer and linguist who worked in Spain in the 1830s as an agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society. Borrow travelled across the country, learned about its people and sought to distribute the New Testament in Spanish. Later he described his experiences in a book entitled The Bible in Spain, which brought the colporteur's adventures to the attention of English readers. His story shows the mixed reception of such efforts: he met with generous hosts and curious readers, but also with obstacles, official suspicion and, at times, imprisonment. In a country where the public practice of any faith other than the Catholic one was restricted, distributing Scripture was not a simple act. It is fair to say that Borrow admired many things about Spain, and that he wrote with affection as well as criticism for the people he met.", "What does this history teach us? First, that a Bible needs a messenger. A printed page does nothing until someone brings it to a reader. Second, that the Church has often advanced through ordinary people, men and women who were not famous but were willing to travel, to knock on doors and to speak. Third, that every society has its own gaps, places where Scripture is scarce or the message has not been heard. The apostle Paul asked in Romans 10:14: how will they call on one in whom they have not believed, and how will they believe in one of whom they have not heard? The colporteurs answered that question with their feet. Today the tools are different, digital, radio and printed, but the question is the same, and the response still begins with a person who goes.", "For this unit you will use language to describe a need and a place. Practise the vocabulary of geography and community (el barrio, el pueblo, la carretera, la biblioteca) and the expressions of necessity and obligation, such as hay que, es necesario y tenemos que. Use the imperfect to describe how the world was (los caminos eran malos, mucha gente no sabía leer) and the preterite to narrate what a colporteur did (salió, caminó, vendió, conversó). In Project Q1, you will apply the same logic to your own city: interview people, identify where Scripture, hope and community are scarce, and write a clear diagnosis. The goal is not to judge anyone, but to see accurately, as a good missionary and a good neighbour should."], "glossary": [{"term": "el colportor", "def": "Colporteur: a travelling distributor of Bibles and Christian literature, on foot, by animal or by ship."}, {"term": "la sociedad bíblica", "def": "Bible society: an organization that translates, prints and distributes Scripture, such as the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804)."}, {"term": "el Evangelio", "def": "Gospel: the good news of Jesus Christ; also one of the four New Testament books about his life."}, {"term": "la brecha", "def": "Gap: a place or group where a need, such as access to Scripture or a Christian witness, is not met."}, {"term": "el camino", "def": "Road or path: central to the colporteur's work because many routes were poor and distances long."}, {"term": "hay que / es necesario", "def": "Impersonal expressions of necessity: 'one must' or 'it is necessary', followed by an infinitive."}, {"term": "el barrio", "def": "Neighbourhood: a local urban area, the starting point for a community diagnosis."}, {"term": "el diagnóstico", "def": "Diagnosis: a careful description of a situation and its needs before proposing action."}], "practice": [{"q": "Why did the Bible societies and colporteurs need each other? Answer in three sentences.", "feedback": "Bible societies produced affordable Scriptures but could not place them in readers' hands without people willing to travel. Colporteurs carried the books where bookshops did not exist. Together they covered production and delivery, which is why the work reached farms, villages and ports."}, {"q": "How does George Borrow's story show that distribution of Scripture received a mixed reception? Give two details.", "feedback": "Borrow met generous hosts and curious readers, but also official suspicion and obstacles, partly because public worship other than Catholic was restricted in Spain. His book The Bible in Spain records both. The mixed reaction teaches us to expect welcome and resistance and to respond with patience and respect."}, {"q": "Describe in Spanish, in four sentences, a colporteur's day. Use two preterite verbs and two imperfect verbs.", "feedback": "Model: 'El colportor salió temprano con su mula. Los caminos eran malos y hacía mucho calor. Llegó a una aldea y vendió tres Biblias. Mucha gente no sabía leer, así que leyó en voz alta.' The preterite narrates completed actions and the imperfect describes the setting and habits."}, {"q": "Name one place in your city that today is 'sin Biblias' or without community support, and propose one respectful way to serve there.", "feedback": "Answers will vary, for example a care home, a new immigrant neighbourhood or a rural town. A strong answer identifies a real need through observation or interview, avoids stereotypes and proposes a service that respects the people, such as friendship, practical help or an invitation, rather than pressure."}], "quiz": [{"q": "When was the British and Foreign Bible Society founded, according to the reading?", "opts": [{"t": "1569", "ok": false}, {"t": "1992", "ok": false}, {"t": "1804", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What is a colporteur?", "opts": [{"t": "A printer's assistant in Basel", "ok": false}, {"t": "A travelling distributor of Bibles and literature", "ok": true}, {"t": "A church treasurer", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What book did George Borrow write about his experience in Spain?", "opts": [{"t": "The Bible in Spain", "ok": true}, {"t": "The Spanish Gospel", "ok": false}, {"t": "The Bear Bible", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which sentence correctly uses the imperfect to describe the setting?", "opts": [{"t": "Los caminos eran malos y mucha gente no sabía leer.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Los caminos fueron malos ayer por la tarde.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Los caminos serán malos y mucha gente no sabe leer.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does Romans 10:14 emphasize?", "opts": [{"t": "Scripture should only be read in Latin", "ok": false}, {"t": "Distribution is unnecessary", "ok": false}, {"t": "People need to hear the message through a messenger", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["British and Foreign Bible Society founded 1804: confirm.", "George Borrow worked in Spain in the 1830s for the BFBS; The Bible in Spain was published in 1843: confirm dates.", "Statement that public non-Catholic worship was restricted in Spain in the 1830s: confirm with a history source.", "Romans 10:14 quotation."], "reading_es": ["El siglo XIX fue una gran época de difusión de la Biblia. La imprenta mejoró, el papel se abarató y los barcos fueron más rápidos. Surgieron entonces las sociedades bíblicas, organizaciones dedicadas a traducir, imprimir y distribuir las Escrituras a un precio asequible. La Sociedad Bíblica Británica y Extranjera, fundada en Londres en 1804, fue una de las más importantes. Gracias a ellas, la Biblia llegó al mundo de habla hispana, desde España hasta las nuevas repúblicas de América del Sur.", "Los colportores eran las personas que llevaban los libros. Caminaban, montaban en mula, viajaban en barco y cargaban Biblias, Evangelios y folletos. Los vendían a bajo precio o los regalaban, y conversaban con quien quisiera escucharlos. El trabajo era cansado y a veces peligroso: los caminos eran malos, las distancias eran enormes y la reacción de la gente variaba entre la indiferencia, la curiosidad, la hospitalidad o la hostilidad. Sin embargo, muchas personas conocieron la Escritura gracias a ellos.", "Un ejemplo famoso es George Borrow, un escritor inglés que trabajó en España en la década de 1830 para la Sociedad Bíblica Británica y Extranjera. Recorrió el país, conoció a su gente y distribuyó el Nuevo Testamento en español. Después contó sus aventuras en el libro La Biblia en España. Su experiencia muestra que la acogida podía ser cálida, pero también que había obstáculos y sospechas, porque en aquel tiempo la práctica pública de otras religiones estaba limitada.", "¿Qué nos enseña esta historia? En primer lugar, que la Biblia necesita mensajeros: una página impresa no hace nada hasta que alguien la lleva a un lector. En segundo lugar, que la Iglesia avanza muchas veces gracias a personas sencillas que viajan, llaman a las puertas y hablan. En tercer lugar, que toda sociedad tiene sus propias brechas, lugares donde la Escritura es escasa. El apóstol Pablo preguntó: ¿cómo creerán en aquel de quien no han oído? (Romanos 10:14). Los colportores respondieron con sus pasos.", "En esta unidad usarás el pretérito para narrar lo que hizo un colportor (salió, caminó, vendió, conversó) y el imperfecto para describir el ambiente (los caminos eran malos, mucha gente no sabía leer). También practicarás expresiones de necesidad, como hay que y es necesario. En el Proyecto Q1 aplicarás esta lógica a tu ciudad: entrevistarás a personas, identificarás dónde falta esperanza o comunidad y escribirás un diagnóstico claro y respetuoso."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:Feb": {"reading": ["Diego Thomson, whose Scottish name was James Thomson, was born in 1788 and died in 1854. He was a teacher who became a travelling missionary of education and Scripture, and his career shows how learning and faith can walk together. In the early nineteenth century, Thomson became interested in a method of teaching known as the Lancasterian system, created by the English educator Joseph Lancaster. In this system, one teacher taught a small number of older or more advanced pupils, who in turn taught groups of younger children, so that a single school could teach hundreds of students at a modest cost. The method was criticised later for being mechanical, but at the time it offered hope to places that had few teachers and few books. Thomson, a Baptist, believed that if people could read, they could read the Scriptures.", "After working in Spain, where he had already seen the importance of the Scriptures for education, Thomson travelled to the Americas in the 1820s, a time when many Hispanic countries were gaining independence from the Spanish crown. His journeys, which took him through Argentina, Chile, Peru, Colombia and other regions, brought him into contact with the new national leaders. Many of these governments wanted to build modern societies with literate citizens, and they were interested in schools. Thomson offered his help in organizing Lancasterian schools. At the same time, he worked as an agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society, promoting the distribution of the New Testament and encouraging its use as a reading text in the classroom. In this way the school and the Bible became partners in the same task.", "Some of the details of Thomson's travels, such as dates and the specific cities he visited, should be confirmed in a reliable history source, but the main outline is clear. He met influential men of the period, and in several places he found officials who welcomed his proposals, since a literate population served the aims of the new republics. Not everyone agreed. Some religious authorities were uneasy about the use of the Bible in school without commentary, and politics changed quickly. Still, Thomson's schools and his Spanish editions of Scripture left an impression in the history of Latin American education. A text that could be used to learn letters and also to learn about God was a powerful combination for a young nation, and it explains why Thomson is still remembered by historians of both education and missions in the region.", "Thomson's life raises a question that missionaries and teachers still ask: is it more important to educate or to proclaim? His example suggests that the question may be false. A school without moral and spiritual foundations can form skilled people without wisdom, and a message without the ability to read can remain locked in the memory of the few. In the book of Deuteronomy, God commanded Israel to speak his words to their children, at home and on the road (Deuteronomy 6:6-7). Teaching the young was always part of faith. At the same time, service must never be a trick. Education offered to win a favour is not true education. A teacher serves the child because the child is valuable, and the message is shared honestly, with freedom for the hearer.", "This unit practises the language of comparison, possibility and advice. You will use conditional forms to express hypotheses and polite suggestions: si tuviéramos más escuelas, los niños podrían leer; yo enseñaría a leer a los vecinos; deberíamos empezar por escuchar. You will compare two ways to serve using expressions such as en cambio, por un lado y por otro lado, and you will cite two sources for your claims. The goal is to speak about history with accuracy and about service with humility. When we compare education and Scripture distribution, we may discover that the best answer is not a choice but a partnership, in which each one strengthens the other."], "glossary": [{"term": "el método lancasteriano", "def": "Lancasterian method: a mutual-instruction system in which advanced pupils teach younger ones under one master."}, {"term": "la escuela", "def": "School: an institution for teaching; Thomson promoted schools using Scripture as a reading text."}, {"term": "el agente", "def": "Agent: a representative of an organization, here of the British and Foreign Bible Society."}, {"term": "la independencia", "def": "Independence: the process by which Hispanic American countries separated from the Spanish crown in the early nineteenth century."}, {"term": "alfabetizar", "def": "To teach to read and write; literacy was a major aim of Thomson's schools."}, {"term": "el condicional", "def": "Conditional tense: the Spanish form (-ía endings) used for hypotheses, polite requests and advice, as in 'enseñaría'."}, {"term": "en cambio", "def": "On the other hand: a connector that contrasts two ideas."}, {"term": "el compañerismo", "def": "Partnership or fellowship: the idea that school and Scripture can work together."}], "practice": [{"q": "Explain in your own words how the Lancasterian method worked and why it was useful in the 1820s.", "feedback": "Advanced pupils taught groups of younger children under the direction of one teacher, so a school could instruct many students at low cost. It was useful where teachers and books were scarce. Critics later called it mechanical, but it gave many children access to reading."}, {"q": "Why did many new Hispanic American leaders welcome Thomson's proposals?", "feedback": "They wanted literate citizens for modern republics and they needed schools. Thomson offered both a method and a text, the New Testament, that could be used to teach reading. Some religious authorities, however, were uneasy, so the reception was mixed."}, {"q": "Use the conditional to write three sentences in Spanish about what you would do to help a community learn to read.", "feedback": "Model: 'Organizaría clases de lectura para los vecinos. Prepararíamos libros sencillos. Los jóvenes enseñarían a los niños.' Check that the verbs end in -ía, -íamos or -ían and that the ideas are realistic and respectful."}, {"q": "Is it more important to educate or to share Scripture? Defend a position in a paragraph and include one counterargument.", "feedback": "A strong answer may argue that they should go together, citing Deuteronomy 6:6-7, while acknowledging that education should never be a trick and that a message needs people who can read. It should respond to a counterargument, such as the claim that schools distract from the message, by pointing out that care for the person is part of the message itself."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Who created the Lancasterian method of teaching?", "opts": [{"t": "Joseph Lancaster", "ok": true}, {"t": "George Borrow", "ok": false}, {"t": "Cipriano de Valera", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What organization did Thomson work for as an agent?", "opts": [{"t": "The Spanish Inquisition", "ok": false}, {"t": "The Basel printers' guild", "ok": false}, {"t": "The British and Foreign Bible Society", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?", "opts": [{"t": "Yo enseñé a leer a los vecinos ayer.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Yo enseñaría a leer a los vecinos.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Yo enseño a leer a los vecinos todos los días.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "In what decade did Thomson travel through Hispanic America, according to the reading?", "opts": [{"t": "1560s", "ok": false}, {"t": "1820s", "ok": true}, {"t": "1990s", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does Deuteronomy 6:6-7 say about teaching?", "opts": [{"t": "Parents should teach God's words to their children", "ok": true}, {"t": "Teaching should be restricted to priests", "ok": false}, {"t": "Schools should avoid religious texts", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["James (Diego) Thomson 1788-1854, Scottish Baptist: confirm denomination and dates.", "Countries and dates visited in the 1820s (Argentina, Chile, Peru, Colombia, Mexico...): confirm itinerary (some sources place his first arrival in Buenos Aires before 1820) and the extent of his stay in Spain before the Americas.", "Joseph Lancaster and the monitorial system: confirm description.", "Claims that some religious authorities objected to Bible reading in schools: confirm with a history source.", "Deuteronomy 6:6-7 quotation."], "reading_es": ["Diego Thomson, cuyo nombre escocés era James Thomson, nació en 1788 y murió en 1854. Era un maestro que se convirtió en misionero itinerante de la educación y de las Escrituras. En el siglo XIX se interesó por el sistema lancasteriano, un método creado por el educador inglés Joseph Lancaster. En él, un maestro enseñaba a los alumnos más avanzados y estos, a su vez, enseñaban a otros niños. Así, una sola escuela podía educar a cientos de estudiantes con poco dinero.", "En la década de 1820, cuando muchos países hispanos se independizaban, Thomson viajó por América del Sur como agente de la Sociedad Bíblica Británica y Extranjera. Visitó países como Argentina, Chile, Perú y Colombia, y conversó con los nuevos dirigentes nacionales, que querían construir sociedades modernas con ciudadanos que supieran leer. Thomson les ofreció su ayuda para organizar escuelas y promovió el Nuevo Testamento como texto de lectura. La escuela y la Biblia trabajaron juntas.", "No todos estaban de acuerdo. Algunas autoridades religiosas desconfiaban del uso de la Biblia en las aulas sin comentarios, y la política cambiaba con rapidez. Sin embargo, el trabajo de Thomson dejó una huella en la historia de la educación latinoamericana. Algunos datos concretos, como fechas y ciudades, deben comprobarse en una fuente histórica fiable.", "La vida de Thomson plantea una pregunta que todavía hoy se hacen los maestros y los misioneros: ¿qué es más importante, educar o anunciar? Su ejemplo sugiere que no hay que elegir. Una escuela sin fundamentos morales puede formar personas hábiles pero sin sabiduría, y un mensaje sin lectura puede quedar en pocos oídos. Deuteronomio 6:6-7 manda enseñar las palabras de Dios a los hijos. Pero el servicio nunca debe ser un truco: se educa al niño porque el niño tiene valor, y el mensaje se comparte con honestidad y libertad.", "En esta unidad practicarás el condicional para expresar hipótesis y sugerencias: si tuviéramos más escuelas, los niños podrían leer; yo enseñaría a leer a los vecinos. También compararás dos formas de servir con expresiones como en cambio y por un lado... por otro lado. La mejor respuesta puede ser una alianza entre educación y Escritura."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:Mar": {"reading": ["After looking at the past, this unit turns to the present. The evangelical church in Spain today is a small but visible part of a society with a long Catholic heritage and an increasingly plural and secularised culture. For most of the twentieth century, Protestants in Spain faced severe limits, and public worship outside the Catholic Church was restricted during the long period of the Franco regime. The restoration of democracy and the constitution of 1978 recognised religious freedom, and in 1992 the Spanish state signed legal cooperation agreements with several religious minorities, including a federation of evangelical churches. These agreements gave evangelical congregations recognised standing in areas such as places of worship, chaplaincy, marriage and teaching of religion. It is a significant step in a long journey from being barely tolerated to being legally acknowledged.", "The federation that signed the agreement is known by its Spanish initials, FEREDE, the Federación de Entidades Religiosas Evangélicas de España. It represents many evangelical churches and entities before the state and speaks on their behalf in matters of law and public policy. Not all evangelical congregations belong to it, and the evangelical world in Spain is diverse: there are Baptist, Brethren, Pentecostal, Reformed and many independent communities, as well as congregations led by immigrants from Latin America, Africa and Eastern Europe. According to figures from FEREDE cited by Chanak, which still need to be verified with an official source, there are approximately 4,400 evangelical places of worship in Spain. This number is useful as an approximation of the scale of the church, but it should always be quoted with its source and a note that it is an estimate.", "What does this landscape tell us? First, that evangelical growth in Spain in recent decades has been closely linked to immigration. Many congregations in large cities are made up largely of newcomers, who bring vibrant worship, strong family networks and a sense of mission, and some of whom plant new churches in neighbourhoods where local Christians are few. Second, that Spanish society is becoming more secular. Many people, especially the young, describe themselves as without religion, which means that churches can no longer assume the cultural familiarity with Christian ideas that they once had. Third, that the relationship between the evangelical minority and the Catholic majority has changed from confrontation to coexistence. Respect, cooperation in social service and honest conversation are possible, and good citizens of every faith benefit from them.", "A church in a neighbourhood has a unique calling: to serve its neighbours. This does not mean to compete, to pressure or to look down on others, but to be present in a way that blesses. A small congregation can run a food bank, offer language classes for immigrants, accompany lonely older people, give homework help to children or simply open its doors for a community meal. Such acts of service demonstrate the character of Christ before words are spoken. At the same time, the Great Commission of Matthew 28:19, which is the verse of this unit, tells believers to go and make disciples of all nations. The two belong together: service without any message can be incomplete, and a message without service can sound empty. The balance requires wisdom, humility and the freedom of the other person to say yes or no.", "In this unit, you will develop your Spanish with the vocabulary of institutions and statistics (la federación, el lugar de culto, la comunidad, la ley, la cifra) and with the expression of sources, using según. Remember that a responsible speaker never presents an estimate as a certainty. Say: según la FEREDE, citada por Chanak, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos, un dato por verificar. In Project Q2, you will design a bilingual outreach kit for a real neighbourhood: a flyer, a short message and an audio script. Make it respectful, accurate and useful, so that anyone who receives it, whatever their beliefs, feels welcomed and informed rather than pressured."], "glossary": [{"term": "la FEREDE", "def": "Spanish initials of the Federation of Evangelical Religious Entities of Spain, which represents many evangelical churches before the state."}, {"term": "el acuerdo de cooperación", "def": "Cooperation agreement: a 1992 legal agreement between the Spanish state and a religious minority, including the evangelical federation."}, {"term": "el lugar de culto", "def": "Place of worship: a building or space where a religious community meets."}, {"term": "la secularización", "def": "Secularisation: the process by which a society becomes less influenced by religious institutions and practices."}, {"term": "la libertad religiosa", "def": "Religious freedom: the right to hold, practise and change one's beliefs; recognised in Spain's 1978 constitution."}, {"term": "según", "def": "According to: a preposition used to attribute information to a source, essential for citing figures responsibly."}, {"term": "la cifra", "def": "Figure or number: a statistic that should be quoted with its source and its level of certainty."}, {"term": "el inmigrante", "def": "Immigrant: a person who has moved to a country from elsewhere; immigrant congregations are growing in many Spanish cities."}], "practice": [{"q": "What is FEREDE and what role does it play, according to the reading?", "feedback": "FEREDE is a federation that represents many evangelical churches and entities before the Spanish state and signed the 1992 cooperation agreement. It speaks on behalf of its members on legal and public matters. The reading adds that not all evangelical congregations belong to it."}, {"q": "How should a student cite the figure of 4,400 places of worship? Write the sentence in Spanish.", "feedback": "Model: 'Según la FEREDE, citada por Chanak, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos en España; es un dato por verificar.' The answer should include the source, the word aproximadamente and a note that the figure must be verified."}, {"q": "Name two ways in which immigration has influenced evangelical churches in Spain. How can a church show respect to all its neighbours?", "feedback": "Immigrants have founded and enlarged many congregations in large cities and have brought strong worship and family networks. A church shows respect by serving without pressure, listening to the neighbourhood's needs, cooperating with others and offering invitations freely. Service should not depend on whether people accept the message."}, {"q": "Describe one practical service a small church could offer in your area, and explain how the Great Commission and respect for others could fit together.", "feedback": "Answers may include a food bank, language classes or visits to the lonely. A good answer explains that serving shows Christ's character, that Matthew 28:19 calls believers to make disciples, and that the message is shared honestly, leaving the other person free to respond."}], "quiz": [{"q": "In what year did the Spanish state sign a cooperation agreement with the evangelical federation, according to the reading?", "opts": [{"t": "1978", "ok": false}, {"t": "1992", "ok": true}, {"t": "1569", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "How should the figure of about 4,400 places of worship be presented?", "opts": [{"t": "As an estimate attributed to FEREDE via Chanak, to be verified", "ok": true}, {"t": "As an exact official count", "ok": false}, {"t": "Without any source", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which sentence correctly cites a source in Spanish?", "opts": [{"t": "Hay 4 400 lugares de culto sin ninguna fuente.", "ok": false}, {"t": "La FEREDE inventó los lugares de culto.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Según la FEREDE, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What has been closely linked to recent evangelical growth in Spain?", "opts": [{"t": "The invention of the printing press", "ok": false}, {"t": "The Lancasterian method", "ok": false}, {"t": "Immigration", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What is the proposed attitude toward the Catholic majority?", "opts": [{"t": "Competition and pressure", "ok": false}, {"t": "Respect, cooperation and honest conversation", "ok": true}, {"t": "Silence and avoidance", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, por verificar: aprox. 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos en España.", "1992 cooperation agreements with evangelical, Jewish and Muslim federations: confirm text and what they cover (worship, chaplaincy, marriage, religion teaching).", "1978 constitution and religious freedom; restrictions under the Franco regime: confirm with an official source.", "FEREDE full name and description of its membership; denominations active in Spain.", "Claims about immigration and secularisation trends: confirm with a recent survey before citing figures."], "reading_es": ["Después de mirar el pasado, esta unidad se centra en el presente. La Iglesia evangélica en España es una minoría pequeña pero visible en una sociedad de larga herencia católica y cada vez más plural y secularizada. Durante gran parte del siglo XX, los protestantes tuvieron serias limitaciones. Con la democracia y la Constitución de 1978 se reconoció la libertad religiosa y, en 1992, el Estado firmó acuerdos de cooperación con varias confesiones minoritarias, entre ellas la federación evangélica.", "La federación que firmó el acuerdo se conoce por sus siglas, FEREDE (Federación de Entidades Religiosas Evangélicas de España). Representa a muchas iglesias y entidades ante el Estado. No todas las congregaciones pertenecen a ella, y el mundo evangélico español es diverso: hay comunidades bautistas, pentecostales, reformadas, de hermanos e independientes, y también muchas iglesias formadas por inmigrantes. Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, que todavía deben verificarse, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos en España. Es una aproximación útil, pero siempre debe citarse con su fuente.", "¿Qué nos dice este panorama? En primer lugar, que el crecimiento evangélico reciente está muy relacionado con la inmigración. En segundo lugar, que la sociedad española es cada vez más secular y que muchos jóvenes se declaran sin religión. En tercer lugar, que la relación entre la minoría evangélica y la mayoría católica ha pasado del enfrentamiento a la convivencia. El respeto, la colaboración en el servicio social y el diálogo sincero son posibles y benefician a todos los ciudadanos.", "Una iglesia de barrio tiene la vocación de servir a sus vecinos. No se trata de competir ni de presionar, sino de bendecir. Una congregación pequeña puede organizar un banco de alimentos, ofrecer clases de español a inmigrantes, acompañar a personas mayores solas o abrir sus puertas para una comida comunitaria. El versículo de la unidad, Mateo 28:19, nos manda a hacer discípulos a todas las naciones. Servicio y mensaje van unidos, siempre con humildad y con libertad para que el otro responda.", "En el Proyecto Q2 diseñarás un kit bilingüe de alcance para un barrio real: un folleto, un mensaje breve y un guion de audio. Recuerda citar las fuentes con la palabra según y no presentar una estimación como una certeza."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:Abr": {"reading": ["A gap is a space between two things that should be connected. In missions, the word describes the distance between a need and the people or resources able to meet it. Last month we studied the evangelical church in Spain as a whole. This month we focus on its absences: the places where, as far as anyone knows, there is no evangelical witness at all. According to figures from FEREDE cited by Chanak, which still need to be verified with an official source, approximately 3,000 Spanish municipalities have no evangelical witness. The number is approximate, and it depends on how the term testimonio is defined: does it count only a registered congregation, or also a home group, a visiting ministry or a single believing family? Before anyone uses the figure in public, that definition must be clarified. Even so, the figure points to a real phenomenon that deserves attention.", "To understand where such municipalities are, we must think about the geography of Spain. In recent decades, many rural areas, especially in the interior of the country, have lost population as young people move to the cities for work and study. Journalists and writers have called this phenomenon la España vaciada, the emptied Spain. In many villages the school has closed, the bakery has closed, the doctor visits twice a week and the church building may be open only for a few services each year. Older residents often remain, with deep roots, strong traditions and sometimes a keen sense of loneliness. Evangelical presence, meanwhile, tends to be concentrated in larger cities and among immigrant communities, so there are many small towns where no evangelical church has ever existed or where an earlier congregation has disappeared.", "It would be a mistake to look at these towns only as problems or as a mission field. They are home to people with names, histories and customs, and many of them already have a rich spiritual life shaped by the Catholic tradition. A responsible approach begins with respect. The questions to ask are not 'how do we convince them?' but 'who lives here, what do they need, and how can we be good neighbours?' Church planting, that is, starting a new congregation, is one response used in missions, but it is rarely the first step. More often, the first step is friendship: visiting, listening, helping with a real need, such as transport to the hospital, shopping for the elderly or computer classes for the young. A long, patient presence builds trust, and trust is the soil in which any message can be heard freely.", "Jesus saw crowds and was moved with compassion because they were like sheep without a shepherd. Then he said to his disciples, in Matthew 9:37-38, that the harvest is plentiful but the workers are few, and he asked them to pray that the Lord of the harvest would send out workers. Notice what he did not say: 'organize a campaign.' He first asked for prayer, which reminds us that mission begins with dependence on God. Then, as the disciples prayed, they themselves became the answer. This is a recurring biblical pattern. The person who sees a gap and feels concern should ask whether he or she is being called to help close it, whether by going, by giving, by praying or by training others who will go.", "In this unit, you will develop the language of persuasion by using the subjunctive in simple forms. Learn these patterns: es importante que visitemos el pueblo; quiero que los vecinos se sientan bienvenidos; ojalá que haya más amigos para ellos; es necesario que escuchemos antes de hablar. Notice that the subjunctive appears after expressions of wish, emotion, doubt or necessity, when the subject changes. A persuasive proposal also needs a source (según la FEREDE), a clear reason and an answer to an objection, such as the cost, the distance or the risk of seeming intrusive. In Project Q2, continue to refine your message after the feedback you received. Let the data move you to concern, but let love, not statistics, guide the plan."], "glossary": [{"term": "la brecha", "def": "Gap: a distance between a need and the people or resources that could meet it; here, places without evangelical witness."}, {"term": "el alcance", "def": "Reach or outreach: the extent to which a message or service arrives at people."}, {"term": "la misión", "def": "Mission: a sending, a task or purpose; in church history, the work of sharing the Gospel and serving others."}, {"term": "el pueblo", "def": "Town or village; also 'a people'. The smaller rural communities are central to this unit."}, {"term": "la España vaciada", "def": "Emptied Spain: a phrase for rural areas of Spain that have lost population in recent decades."}, {"term": "el testimonio", "def": "Witness or testimony: in the FEREDE figure, the presence of an evangelical community or congregation, a term whose definition must be clarified."}, {"term": "el subjuntivo", "def": "Subjunctive mood: a verb form used after expressions of wish, emotion, doubt or necessity, as in 'quiero que vengan'."}, {"term": "la mies", "def": "Harvest: a biblical image in Matthew 9:37-38 for people ready to hear and the work of reaching them."}], "practice": [{"q": "Why must the 3,000-municipality figure be handled carefully? Mention at least two reasons.", "feedback": "It is an estimate cited from FEREDE through Chanak and still needs verification. It also depends on how testimonio is defined: a registered congregation, a home group or a single family could be counted differently. Presenting an unverified figure as certain would weaken the credibility of the project."}, {"q": "What does 'España vaciada' mean, and how does it relate to the gap in evangelical witness?", "feedback": "It refers to rural areas of Spain that have lost population as young people move to cities. Services close, residents are often elderly, and evangelical presence tends to concentrate in cities and immigrant communities. As a result, many small towns have no evangelical congregation."}, {"q": "Write three sentences in Spanish using the subjunctive to propose how to help a small town.", "feedback": "Model: 'Es importante que visitemos a las personas mayores. Quiero que los vecinos se sientan bienvenidos. Ojalá que podamos ofrecer clases de informática.' Verify that each sentence has a main clause of wish, emotion or necessity and a second clause in the subjunctive with a different subject."}, {"q": "According to the reading, what should come before church planting? Explain why this matters for respect.", "feedback": "Friendship and service come first: listening, visiting and helping with real needs. This matters because trust is built slowly and because people have the right to hear a message freely, not under pressure. Respect also means recognising the existing spiritual life and traditions of the town."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What figure does the reading attribute to FEREDE, cited by Chanak, about municipalities?", "opts": [{"t": "Approximately 300 churches in Madrid", "ok": false}, {"t": "Exactly 8,000 missionaries", "ok": false}, {"t": "Approximately 3,000 municipalities without evangelical witness", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What does 'la España vaciada' describe?", "opts": [{"t": "A cathedral in Seville", "ok": false}, {"t": "Rural areas that have lost population", "ok": true}, {"t": "A Bible translation", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which sentence uses the subjunctive correctly?", "opts": [{"t": "Quiero que los vecinos se sientan bienvenidos.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Quiero que los vecinos se sienten bienvenidos.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Quiero que los vecinos son bienvenidos.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "According to Matthew 9:37-38, what did Jesus ask his disciples to do?", "opts": [{"t": "Pray that the Lord of the harvest send workers", "ok": true}, {"t": "Build a large church immediately", "ok": false}, {"t": "Wait until the harvest was smaller", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What is the recommended first step in a town with no evangelical presence?", "opts": [{"t": "A public campaign to convince people", "ok": false}, {"t": "Ignoring the town's traditions", "ok": false}, {"t": "Friendship and service based on listening", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, por verificar: aprox. 3 000 municipios sin testimonio evangélico; confirm the definition of 'testimonio' before publishing.", "Spain's rural depopulation and 'España vaciada': confirm with an official or academic source before citing numbers.", "Statement that evangelical presence is concentrated in large cities and among immigrant communities: confirm.", "Matthew 9:36-38 quotation."], "reading_es": ["Una brecha es un espacio entre dos cosas que deberían estar conectadas. En misiones, la palabra describe la distancia entre una necesidad y las personas capaces de atenderla. Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, que todavía deben verificarse, aproximadamente 3 000 municipios españoles no tienen testimonio evangélico. La cifra es aproximada y depende de cómo se defina testimonio: si cuenta solo una congregación registrada o también un grupo en un hogar, un ministerio itinerante o una familia creyente. Antes de usarla en público, hay que aclarar esa definición.", "Para entender dónde están estos municipios, debemos pensar en la geografía de España. En las últimas décadas, muchas zonas rurales del interior han perdido población porque los jóvenes se marchan a las ciudades. A este fenómeno se le llama la España vaciada. En muchos pueblos han cerrado la escuela y la panadería, el médico llega dos días por semana y la iglesia abre solo en algunas fechas. Quedan personas mayores con raíces profundas, tradiciones fuertes y, a veces, mucha soledad. La presencia evangélica se concentra en las ciudades grandes y en las comunidades inmigrantes.", "Sería un error mirar esos pueblos solo como un problema o como un campo de misión. Son hogares de personas con nombres, historia y costumbres, y muchas tienen una vida espiritual rica, marcada por la tradición católica. Un enfoque responsable comienza con respeto. Las preguntas no son cómo convencerlos, sino quién vive aquí, qué necesita y cómo podemos ser buenos vecinos. La plantación de iglesias es una respuesta posible, pero rara vez es el primer paso. Primero viene la amistad: visitar, escuchar y ayudar con necesidades reales.", "Jesús vio a las multitudes y sintió compasión. Después dijo a sus discípulos: la mies es mucha, mas los obreros son pocos; rogad, pues, al Señor de la mies que envíe obreros a su mies (Mateo 9:37-38). Primero pidió oración, porque la misión empieza con dependencia de Dios. Luego los discípulos se convirtieron en la respuesta. Quien ve una brecha debe preguntarse si Dios lo llama a ir, a dar, a orar o a formar a otros.", "En esta unidad practicarás el subjuntivo para persuadir: es importante que visitemos el pueblo; quiero que los vecinos se sientan bienvenidos; ojalá que haya más amigos para ellos. Una propuesta persuasiva necesita una fuente, una razón clara y una respuesta a una objeción. Que los datos te muevan a la preocupación, pero que el amor guíe el plan."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:May": {"reading": ["Latin America has changed religiously during the last century. For several hundred years, the dominant Christian tradition in the region was Roman Catholic, and this heritage continues to shape languages, festivals, family life and public culture across the continent. Alongside it, evangelical and Pentecostal churches have grown remarkably, especially from the twentieth century. According to estimates cited by Chanak, which vary greatly from country to country and must be verified before they are used with precision, the evangelical share of the population rose from a very small proportion, around 2 percent historically, to somewhere between 20 and 40 percent in recent decades in some places. Because the range is wide and depends on the country, the survey and the definition of evangelical, this unit does not give figures for individual countries. The safest statement is that growth has been substantial and uneven.", "The beginnings of modern Pentecostalism are commonly traced to the Azusa Street revival in Los Angeles in 1906, a gathering led by William Seymour, an African American preacher, in which people reported experiences of the Holy Spirit and the movement spread quickly through letters, travellers and missionaries. In Latin America, Pentecostal communities arose in the early twentieth century. A well-known case is Chile, where a revival among Methodists in Valparaíso around 1909 gave rise to a Pentecostal church with strong local leadership. In Brazil, Pentecostal missions began in the 1910s, linked to immigrants and missionaries, and spread across the country. From these starting points, Pentecostal and charismatic churches expanded through many countries, often led by nationals rather than foreign missionaries, and often among working-class and rural populations.", "Another important event was the 1916 Congress on Christian Work in Latin America, held in Panama. Mission leaders from several countries met to discuss cooperation, avoid duplication and think about education, literature and training. Whatever one thinks of its decisions, the congress shows that missions at that time were considering how to work together and how to prepare local leaders. In the following decades, evangelical churches in many Latin American countries grew notably, and leadership increasingly passed into local hands. Historians offer several explanations for the growth. Some stress the appeal of a personal faith, vivid worship and a strong sense of community. Others point to rapid urbanisation, which left many migrants to the cities looking for belonging, and to the ability of Pentecostal churches to form leaders from among ordinary people.", "An honest analysis should also include counterarguments and limits. Growth in numbers does not always equal depth of discipleship, and some critics argue that rapid expansion has sometimes produced shallow teaching or division. Social scientists also remind us that the Catholic Church remains the largest Christian body in the region and that many of its members are deeply committed, so that talk of competition would be both inaccurate and unkind. In many countries, Catholics and evangelicals work together on social issues such as poverty, education and the care of families. Still, the question of what makes a church grow is worth asking. Programs matter, people matter and the work of the Holy Spirit matters. Acts 2:17, the verse of this unit, records the promise that God would pour out his Spirit on all flesh, young and old, men and women. Whatever human explanations we offer, the Bible reminds us that growth is finally a gift.", "This unit practises the language of comparison and cause and effect. Use expressions such as más que, menos que, mientras que and en cambio to compare, and por eso, debido a and a causa de to explain consequences. For example: las iglesias pentecostales crecieron debido a que formaron líderes locales; por eso muchas comunidades se multiplicaron rápidamente. You will deliver an oral analysis with two causes and one counterargument, and begin to outline your final project plan for Q3. Remember to avoid unsupported numbers: when you do not have a verified source, say that growth was significant but varies by country."], "glossary": [{"term": "el avivamiento", "def": "Revival: a period of renewed spiritual interest and intensity, such as Azusa Street (1906)."}, {"term": "el crecimiento", "def": "Growth: increase in numbers or influence; evangelical growth in Latin America is large but varies by country."}, {"term": "la denominación", "def": "Denomination: a family or association of churches with shared doctrine and organization."}, {"term": "el pentecostal", "def": "Pentecostal: a Christian who emphasises the work of the Holy Spirit, spiritual gifts and vivid worship."}, {"term": "el misionero", "def": "Missionary: a person sent to share the Gospel and serve in another place or culture."}, {"term": "el discipulado", "def": "Discipleship: the long-term process of teaching and forming followers of Christ."}, {"term": "mientras que / en cambio", "def": "While / on the other hand: connectors used to contrast two realities."}, {"term": "debido a / por eso", "def": "Due to / for that reason: expressions that state cause and effect."}], "practice": [{"q": "How should a student present the claim that evangelical growth went from about 2 percent to 20-40 percent? Why is no figure given for individual countries?", "feedback": "The claim should be attributed to estimates cited by Chanak and described as approximate and to be verified. The range is wide because growth varies greatly by country, survey method and definition of evangelical. Without verified sources it would be irresponsible to state country figures."}, {"q": "Name two events or movements in the reading that help explain the growth of evangelical churches in Latin America.", "feedback": "Possible answers: the Azusa Street revival (1906) and the spread of Pentecostalism; the early Pentecostal communities in Chile (c. 1909) and Brazil (1910s); and the 1916 Panama Congress on cooperation among missions. Each shows how movements, missionaries and local leaders combined."}, {"q": "Write three Spanish sentences that compare or explain causes, using mientras que, en cambio or debido a.", "feedback": "Model: 'Las iglesias pentecostales formaron líderes locales, mientras que otras dependían de misioneros extranjeros.' 'Muchos migrantes buscaban comunidad; por eso las congregaciones crecieron.' 'El crecimiento fue rápido debido a la urbanización.' Check connectors and verb agreement."}, {"q": "Give one counterargument to the idea that numerical growth proves the health of a church, and answer it.", "feedback": "A counterargument: growth may hide shallow teaching or division. An answer could affirm that numbers show interest but discipleship needs depth, which is why training pastors and teaching are priorities. Acts 2:17 reminds us that growth is a gift, not only a result of method."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What event of 1906 is commonly seen as a starting point of modern Pentecostalism?", "opts": [{"t": "The Azusa Street revival", "ok": true}, {"t": "The Panama Congress", "ok": false}, {"t": "The Basel printing", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Why does this unit avoid country-by-country percentages?", "opts": [{"t": "Because Latin America has no evangelicals", "ok": false}, {"t": "Because the Catholic Church forbids numbers", "ok": false}, {"t": "Estimates vary widely and need verification", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence correctly expresses cause and effect?", "opts": [{"t": "Las iglesias crecieron en cambio de migrantes.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Muchos migrantes buscaban comunidad; por eso las iglesias crecieron.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Por eso los migrantes, las iglesias.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What did the 1916 Panama Congress encourage?", "opts": [{"t": "The ban of all missions", "ok": false}, {"t": "Cooperation among missions in Latin America", "ok": true}, {"t": "The printing of the Bear Bible", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does the reading say about the Catholic Church in Latin America?", "opts": [{"t": "It remains the largest Christian body and is often a partner on social issues", "ok": true}, {"t": "It disappeared in the twentieth century", "ok": false}, {"t": "It is the enemy of every other church", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Según estimaciones citadas por Chanak, por verificar: crecimiento evangélico en Latinoamérica del 2 % histórico al 20-40 % actual; varía mucho por país; no se dan cifras por país.", "Azusa Street 1906 and William Seymour: confirm.", "Chile Pentecostal beginnings around 1909 (Valparaíso, Methodist background) and Brazil in the 1910s: confirm dates and names with a church history source.", "1916 Panama Congress on Christian Work in Latin America: confirm date and aims.", "Statement that the Catholic Church is the largest Christian body in the region: confirm with a recent source."], "reading_es": ["Latinoamérica ha cambiado en lo religioso durante el último siglo. Durante siglos, la tradición cristiana dominante fue la católica, y esa herencia sigue marcando los idiomas, las fiestas y la vida familiar. Al mismo tiempo, las iglesias evangélicas y pentecostales han crecido de manera notable desde el siglo XX. Según estimaciones citadas por Chanak, que varían mucho de un país a otro y deben verificarse, la proporción evangélica pasó de un 2 % histórico a entre un 20 % y un 40 % en algunos lugares. Como el rango es amplio, en esta unidad no damos cifras por país.", "El pentecostalismo moderno se asocia comúnmente con el avivamiento de la calle Azusa, en Los Ángeles, en 1906. En Latinoamérica surgieron comunidades pentecostales a comienzos del siglo XX. En Chile, un avivamiento entre metodistas en Valparaíso hacia 1909 dio origen a una iglesia pentecostal con fuerte liderazgo local. En Brasil, las misiones pentecostales comenzaron en la década de 1910. Desde allí el movimiento se extendió por el continente, con líderes del propio país y muchas veces entre poblaciones rurales y trabajadoras.", "Otro hecho importante fue el Congreso sobre la Obra Cristiana en América Latina, celebrado en Panamá en 1916. Líderes misioneros de varios países se reunieron para hablar de cooperación, educación y formación de líderes. En las décadas siguientes, las iglesias evangélicas crecieron en muchos países y el liderazgo pasó cada vez más a manos locales. Los historiadores señalan varias causas: una fe personal, un culto vivo, la comunidad y la urbanización, que dejó a muchos migrantes buscando pertenencia.", "Un análisis honesto incluye también límites. El crecimiento numérico no siempre significa profundidad en el discipulado, y la Iglesia católica sigue siendo la mayor comunidad cristiana de la región, con muchos miembros comprometidos. En numerosos países, católicos y evangélicos colaboran en asuntos sociales como la pobreza y la educación. Hechos 2:17 recuerda la promesa de que Dios derramaría su Espíritu sobre toda carne. Más allá de las explicaciones humanas, el crecimiento es finalmente un don.", "En esta unidad compararás con expresiones como más que, menos que, mientras que y en cambio, y explicarás causas con por eso y debido a. Por ejemplo: las iglesias crecieron debido a que formaron líderes locales; por eso muchas comunidades se multiplicaron. Cuando no tengas una fuente verificada, di que el crecimiento fue significativo, pero que varía según el país."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:Jun": {"reading": ["When people study Christianity in Latin America and Spain, they quickly discover that there is not one evangelical church but many. A useful way to organise this variety is by denominational family, a group of churches that share a history, a doctrine and a way of governing themselves. This unit introduces four large families. They are the historic Protestant churches, the Reformed and Presbyterian family, the Baptist family and the Pentecostal family. These categories overlap and every country has its own particular mixture, so it is wise not to assign dates or numbers to specific countries before consulting a reliable history. What can be said safely is the general outline, which helps us understand the strengths and the challenges of the church today.", "Historic Protestant missions, such as the Methodist, Presbyterian, Baptist and Anglican, arrived in Latin America mainly from the nineteenth century, often after political changes made it easier to enter. These missions did much more than found congregations. They opened schools, which taught reading and mathematics to children who would otherwise have had no education. They founded publishing houses that printed hymnals, Bibles and books. They also built clinics and hospitals. In this sense, the missionary movement was a movement of service, and many national institutions of education and health have roots in it. The Reformed and Presbyterian tradition emphasises the sovereignty of God, the authority of Scripture and careful teaching, while the Baptist tradition emphasises believer's baptism, the independence of each congregation and a strong missionary commitment.", "Pentecostal churches grew alongside them, with strong lay and local leadership. Ordinary believers, often without formal theological education, preached, prayed for the sick, led worship and started new congregations. Their rapid expansion owed much to a culture of testimony, to services that were participatory and joyful, and to leaders who were part of the same communities as the people they served. In Spain, some Pentecostal and independent congregations are also closely related to the Roma community and to immigrant networks. Historians note that these families are not enemies of one another. They share a commitment to Christ and to the Bible, even where they differ on baptism, church government or spiritual gifts.", "With growth come challenges. The most common are the training of pastors, the care of new believers, doctrinal teaching and unity among churches. Many congregations have a pastor who works another job and has had little access to theological study, so training must be offered in forms that are accessible and affordable. New believers need patient accompaniment, since conversion is a beginning and not an end. Teaching must be sound so that communities are not carried away by every new idea, and unity is difficult when denominations compete or fail to know one another. Jesus prayed in John 17 that his followers would be one, so that the world might believe. A divided church tells a confusing story, and a cooperating church tells a credible one. Practical forms of unity are modest and possible: pastors who pray together, shared training courses, joint service to the poor and a willingness to speak well of other congregations. Such habits do not erase real differences, but they show that disagreement can coexist with love.", "The verse for this unit, Ephesians 4:12, states that Christ gave leaders to equip the saints for the work of ministry and for the building up of the body of Christ. Notice that the leader's task is to equip, not to do everything alone. In this way discipleship becomes a long journey of the whole community. Is it harder to win a person or to disciple them for life? Most pastors answer that discipleship lasts longer and requires more patience. In your Spanish work, practise the language of advice and commands: ten paciencia, escucha primero, ayuda a los nuevos, no dividas la iglesia; le recomiendo que forme líderes. You will conduct a recorded interview on discipleship challenges and use at least eight advice or command forms accurately."], "glossary": [{"term": "la denominación", "def": "Denomination: a family of churches that share doctrine, history and organization."}, {"term": "histórico / histórica", "def": "Historic: refers to the Protestant churches (Methodist, Presbyterian, Baptist, Anglican) that arrived mainly from the nineteenth century."}, {"term": "reformado / presbiteriano", "def": "Reformed or Presbyterian: a tradition that emphasises God's sovereignty, Scripture and careful doctrine, and is governed by elders."}, {"term": "bautista", "def": "Baptist: a tradition that emphasises believer's baptism and the independence of each local congregation."}, {"term": "el laico", "def": "Lay person: a believer who is not ordained clergy; Pentecostal growth relied heavily on lay leaders."}, {"term": "el discipulado", "def": "Discipleship: the lifelong process of teaching, forming and accompanying followers of Christ."}, {"term": "la unidad", "def": "Unity: the oneness for which Jesus prayed in John 17."}, {"term": "el imperativo", "def": "Imperative mood: the command form in Spanish, used for advice such as 'ten paciencia' or 'escucha primero'."}], "practice": [{"q": "Name two contributions of the historic Protestant missions beyond founding churches.", "feedback": "They opened schools, founded publishing houses and built clinics and hospitals. These institutions helped education, literacy and health, and many have lasting roots in national life. They show mission as service as well as proclamation."}, {"q": "What is the main strength of Pentecostal growth according to the reading, and what challenge may accompany it?", "feedback": "A strength is strong lay and local leadership: ordinary believers served as pastors, evangelists and worship leaders. A challenge can be training and doctrinal teaching, because leaders may have little formal education and rapid growth can outpace teaching. The reading says these challenges apply to many churches, not only one family."}, {"q": "Write four pieces of advice in Spanish, two with the command form and two with 'le recomiendo que...' plus the subjunctive, for a young pastor.", "feedback": "Model: 'Ten paciencia con los nuevos creyentes.' 'Escucha antes de aconsejar.' 'Le recomiendo que forme a otros líderes.' 'Le recomiendo que busque unidad con las iglesias vecinas.' Check the accent on tú commands, such as ten, and subjunctive endings."}, {"q": "Is it harder to win a person for Christ or to disciple that person for life? Defend your position with one reason from the reading.", "feedback": "A strong answer may argue that discipleship is harder because it lasts a lifetime and requires patience, teaching and community, citing the challenges of training, care of new believers and unity. It may acknowledge that the first step matters too. Ephesians 4:12 shows that discipling is the work of the whole body, not of one person."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What do historic Protestant missions share with Pentecostal churches according to the reading?", "opts": [{"t": "The same church government", "ok": false}, {"t": "A commitment to Christ and the Bible", "ok": true}, {"t": "The same founding date", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which of the following was NOT mentioned as an institution founded by historic missions?", "opts": [{"t": "Casinos", "ok": true}, {"t": "Schools", "ok": false}, {"t": "Publishing houses", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does Ephesians 4:12 say is the task of Christian leaders?", "opts": [{"t": "To avoid teaching", "ok": false}, {"t": "To work alone", "ok": false}, {"t": "To equip the saints for the work of ministry", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence uses the imperative correctly?", "opts": [{"t": "Tienes paciencia con los nuevos creyentes ayer.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Tendrás paciencia con los nuevos creyentes ayer.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Ten paciencia con los nuevos creyentes.", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Why does the reading advise against attaching specific dates and numbers to individual countries without sources?", "opts": [{"t": "Because denominations do not exist", "ok": false}, {"t": "Each country has its own mixture and the facts need verification", "ok": true}, {"t": "Because all countries are identical", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Confirm which denominations were established in which countries before naming specific dates in class.", "Descriptions of Reformed/Presbyterian and Baptist distinctives: confirm with denominational sources.", "Statement that historic missions founded schools, publishing houses and clinics: confirm with examples.", "Link between Pentecostal and independent congregations and Roma or immigrant communities in Spain: confirm with a source.", "John 17 and Ephesians 4:12 quotations."], "reading_es": ["Cuando se estudia el cristianismo en Latinoamérica y España, se descubre que no hay una sola iglesia evangélica, sino muchas. Una forma útil de ordenar esta variedad es por familias denominacionales: grupos de iglesias con historia, doctrina y gobierno comunes. En esta unidad conocemos cuatro: las históricas, las reformadas y presbiterianas, las bautistas y las pentecostales. Estas categorías se superponen y cada país tiene su propia mezcla, así que no conviene asignar fechas ni cifras a países concretos sin consultar una fuente fiable.", "Las misiones protestantes históricas, como la metodista, la presbiteriana, la bautista y la anglicana, llegaron a Latinoamérica sobre todo a partir del siglo XIX. No solo fundaron iglesias, sino también escuelas, editoriales, clínicas y hospitales. La tradición reformada y presbiteriana destaca la soberanía de Dios, la autoridad de la Escritura y la enseñanza cuidadosa. La tradición bautista subraya el bautismo de los creyentes, la independencia de cada congregación y un fuerte compromiso misionero.", "Las iglesias pentecostales crecieron al mismo tiempo, con un liderazgo laico y local muy fuerte. Creyentes sencillos predicaban, oraban por los enfermos, dirigían la alabanza y abrían nuevas congregaciones. Su expansión se debió a una cultura del testimonio, a cultos participativos y alegres y a líderes que pertenecían a las mismas comunidades. Estas familias no son enemigas. Comparten el compromiso con Cristo y con la Biblia, aunque difieran en el bautismo, el gobierno de la iglesia o los dones espirituales.", "El crecimiento trae retos. Los más comunes son la formación de pastores, el cuidado de los nuevos creyentes, la enseñanza doctrinal y la unidad entre iglesias. Muchos pastores trabajan en otro empleo y han tenido poco acceso a estudios teológicos. Los nuevos creyentes necesitan acompañamiento paciente, porque la conversión es un comienzo. Jesús oró en Juan 17 para que sus seguidores fueran uno, a fin de que el mundo creyera.", "Efesios 4:12 dice que Cristo dio líderes para perfeccionar a los santos para la obra del ministerio. El líder debe equipar, no hacerlo todo solo. En español practicarás consejos y mandatos: ten paciencia, escucha primero, ayuda a los nuevos, no dividas la iglesia; le recomiendo que forme líderes. Harás una entrevista sobre los retos del discipulado y usarás al menos ocho formas de consejo o mandato."]}, "us:world_language:conversational:Jul": {"reading": ["This final unit brings the year together. We began in sixteenth-century Spain, where a monk named Casiodoro de Reina left his monastery and his country and, after years of exile, saw a complete Bible in Castilian printed in Basel in 1569, the Biblia del Oso. We followed Cipriano de Valera, whose revision published in Amsterdam in 1602 gave the tradition its double name, Reina-Valera. We learned that these men worked in a tense world, in which the Inquisition, founded in 1478, prosecuted small groups of reform-minded believers in Seville and Valladolid in the 1550s and 1560s. We also learned to speak about that history with truth and without hatred toward people alive today, whatever their tradition. Each step showed that one translation, patiently made and carefully revised, can serve generations.", "In the nineteenth century, the story moved across the Atlantic. Bible societies such as the British and Foreign Bible Society, founded in 1804, supported the printing and distribution of Scripture, and colporteurs carried books on foot, by mule and by ship. George Borrow worked in Spain in the 1830s and wrote The Bible in Spain. Diego, or James, Thomson, who lived from 1788 to 1854, promoted Lancasterian schools and the use of the New Testament as a reading text in the young Hispanic republics of the 1820s. From these chapters we draw a lesson that remains useful: Scripture and education, words and service, often travel together, and every movement depends on ordinary people who are willing to go.", "Then we turned to the present. In Spain, according to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak and still to be verified, there are approximately 4,400 evangelical places of worship, and approximately 3,000 municipalities have no evangelical witness, a gap sharpened by the phenomenon of the emptied Spain. In Latin America, according to estimates cited by Chanak, evangelical growth went from about 2 percent historically to between 20 and 40 percent today, with great differences from one country to another. We met Pentecostal, Baptist, Reformed and historic families, and we saw that the greatest challenges are training pastors, caring for new believers, teaching sound doctrine and cultivating unity. Throughout, we tried to treat the Catholic majority and all peoples with respect, recognising what we share and speaking of differences honestly.", "Your final project, La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar, asks you to apply everything to a real place. A complete plan has five parts: a diagnosis of the gap (who lives there, where, what they need), a message and resources (what will be said and offered, and in what form), a timeline, a budget and a method of evaluation. It will be assessed with the Chanak rubric: 30 percent for knowledge, which includes the history, the data with sources and the vocabulary; 40 percent for execution, which includes the clarity and feasibility of the plan and the quality of the oral presentation in Spanish; and 30 percent for local connection, which means evidence that you listened to real people and that your plan fits their situation. Notice that the largest weight falls on execution. A well-informed student who cannot present a workable plan has not yet completed the task.", "Acts 1:8 gives the unit its verse: you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem, in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth. The pattern moves outward from the local to the distant, and it begins at home. For your presentation, remember a few principles. Cite every figure with its source and say when it is an estimate. Use past tenses to narrate the history, the subjunctive to propose and persuade, the conditional to imagine and soften, and connectors to organise your argument. Answer objections with respect and allow questions at the end. Finally, if you could speak to Reina, Valera or Thomson today, what would you tell them about your plan? Perhaps you would say that the Book they cherished is still the lamp for our feet, and that the gap is now our task to close with humility, patience and love."], "glossary": [{"term": "la brecha", "def": "Gap: the distance between a need and the people or resources that can meet it; the core idea of the final project."}, {"term": "el alcance", "def": "Outreach or reach: how far a message or service arrives, and the second half of the project title."}, {"term": "el diagnóstico", "def": "Diagnosis: a careful description of a place, its people and its needs, supported by interviews and sources."}, {"term": "el cronograma", "def": "Timeline or schedule: an ordered plan of actions and dates."}, {"term": "el presupuesto", "def": "Budget: an estimate of the money and resources needed to carry out a plan."}, {"term": "la evaluación", "def": "Evaluation: the method by which a project's results are measured and improved."}, {"term": "la rúbrica", "def": "Rubric: a scoring guide; the Chanak rubric weights 30 percent knowledge, 40 percent execution and 30 percent local connection."}, {"term": "la conexión local", "def": "Local connection: evidence that a plan is based on listening to real people in a real place."}], "practice": [{"q": "Summarise in three sentences how the Reina-Valera tradition began.", "feedback": "Casiodoro de Reina, a former monk of San Isidoro del Campo, translated the whole Bible into Castilian during his exile and saw it printed in Basel in 1569 as the Biblia del Oso. Cipriano de Valera revised that text and published it in Amsterdam in 1602. The combined names give the Reina-Valera tradition, still used in many Spanish-speaking churches."}, {"q": "Explain the Chanak rubric for the final project and say which part has the greatest weight and why.", "feedback": "The rubric assigns 30 percent to knowledge (history, sourced data and vocabulary), 40 percent to execution (the feasibility and clarity of the plan and the quality of the presentation) and 30 percent to local connection (evidence of listening to real people). Execution has the greatest weight because a plan must be workable and well communicated, not only well informed."}, {"q": "Write two Spanish sentences that cite the FEREDE figures correctly, and explain why you chose that wording.", "feedback": "Model: 'Según la FEREDE, citada por Chanak, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos en España; es un dato por verificar.' 'Según la misma fuente, unos 3 000 municipios no tienen testimonio evangélico.' The wording names the source, uses approximation words and signals that the figures must be checked."}, {"q": "If you could talk to Reina, Valera or Thomson, what would you tell them about your plan? Answer in a short paragraph that includes one past-tense sentence and one subjunctive sentence.", "feedback": "Answers will vary. A strong paragraph connects the historical figure's work to the plan, for example: 'Reina tradujo la Biblia para que su pueblo la leyera; quiero que los vecinos de mi barrio se sientan bienvenidos.' It shows accurate use of the preterite and the subjunctive and a humble tone."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What percentage of the final project grade is assigned to execution in the Chanak rubric?", "opts": [{"t": "30 percent", "ok": false}, {"t": "60 percent", "ok": false}, {"t": "40 percent", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What is the correct pairing of translators and years?", "opts": [{"t": "Reina 1602 and Valera 1569", "ok": false}, {"t": "Reina 1569 and Valera 1602", "ok": true}, {"t": "Reina 1478 and Valera 1804", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "How should the figure of 3,000 municipalities without evangelical witness be presented?", "opts": [{"t": "As an approximate figure from FEREDE cited by Chanak, to be verified", "ok": true}, {"t": "As a verified government count", "ok": false}, {"t": "As a prediction for next year", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which sentence uses the subjunctive to propose?", "opts": [{"t": "Es importante que visitemos el pueblo.", "ok": true}, {"t": "Es importante que visitamos el pueblo.", "ok": false}, {"t": "Es importante visitaremos el pueblo.", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What pattern does Acts 1:8 describe for witness?", "opts": [{"t": "From a distant land inward to Jerusalem", "ok": false}, {"t": "Only within one's own family", "ok": false}, {"t": "From Jerusalem outward to the ends of the earth", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, por verificar: aprox. 4 400 lugares de culto y aprox. 3 000 municipios sin testimonio evangélico; reconfirm before the final presentation is used publicly.", "Según estimaciones citadas por Chanak, por verificar: crecimiento evangélico de 2 % a 20-40 %; varía por país.", "Dates in the synthesis: Reina's Bible 1569 (Basel), Valera's revision 1602 (Amsterdam), Inquisition 1478, BFBS 1804, Thomson 1788-1854, Borrow 1830s.", "Chanak rubric weights (30 % knowledge, 40 % execution, 30 % local connection) as applied by the team.", "Acts 1:8 quotation."], "reading_es": ["Esta última unidad reúne todo el año. Empezamos en la España del siglo XVI, donde el monje Casiodoro de Reina salió de su monasterio y de su país y, tras años de exilio, vio impresa en Basilea en 1569 una Biblia completa en castellano: la Biblia del Oso. Seguimos a Cipriano de Valera, cuya revisión publicada en Ámsterdam en 1602 dio nombre a la tradición Reina-Valera. Aprendimos que estos hombres vivieron en un mundo tenso, en el que la Inquisición, fundada en 1478, procesó a pequeños grupos de creyentes reformados en Sevilla y Valladolid. También aprendimos a hablar de esa historia con verdad y sin odio.", "En el siglo XIX la historia cruzó el Atlántico. Las sociedades bíblicas, como la Sociedad Bíblica Británica y Extranjera, fundada en 1804, apoyaron la impresión y distribución de las Escrituras, y los colportores las llevaron a pie, en mula y en barco. George Borrow trabajó en España en la década de 1830 y escribió La Biblia en España. Diego Thomson, que vivió de 1788 a 1854, promovió las escuelas lancasterianas y el uso del Nuevo Testamento como texto de lectura en las jóvenes repúblicas hispanas. De estos capítulos aprendemos que la Escritura y la educación suelen caminar juntas.", "Después miramos el presente. En España, según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak y todavía por verificar, hay aproximadamente 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos y unos 3 000 municipios sin testimonio evangélico. En Latinoamérica, según estimaciones citadas por Chanak, el crecimiento evangélico pasó de un 2 % histórico a entre un 20 % y un 40 % actual, con grandes diferencias entre países. Conocimos las familias pentecostal, bautista, reformada e histórica, y vimos que los retos principales son formar pastores, cuidar a los nuevos creyentes, enseñar doctrina sana y cultivar la unidad.", "Tu proyecto final, La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar, te pide aplicar todo a un lugar real. Un plan completo tiene cinco partes: diagnóstico de la brecha, mensaje y recursos, cronograma, presupuesto y método de evaluación. La rúbrica de Chanak valora un 30 % el conocimiento, un 40 % la ejecución y un 30 % la conexión local. Fíjate en que el mayor peso recae en la ejecución: un buen estudiante debe presentar un plan realizable.", "Hechos 1:8 es el versículo de la unidad: me seréis testigos en Jerusalén, en toda Judea, en Samaria y hasta lo último de la tierra. El patrón va de lo local a lo lejano. En tu presentación, cita cada cifra con su fuente, usa los pasados para narrar la historia, el subjuntivo para proponer y el condicional para sugerir. Si pudieras hablar hoy con Reina, Valera o Thomson, ¿qué les contarías sobre tu plan?"]}});
